    id: "re-noi-025",
    track: "re",
    module: "re-noi",
    topic: "Insurance",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "How do you underwrite insurance on an acquisition, and why can the seller's cost mislead?",
    a: "Get a quote for the coverage you'll actually carry rather than rolling the seller's number forward. Premiums depend on replacement cost, location and catastrophe exposure (wind, flood, wildfire, earthquake), construction type, deductibles and loss history. A seller's cost can be low because of an old policy, a portfolio-wide program or thinner coverage than your lender will require.",
    why: "Property coverage is priced on what it would cost to rebuild, not on market value, so two buildings worth very different amounts can cost similar sums to insure. Location drives most of the variation: exposure to coastal storms or wildfire can multiply premiums. Deductibles are a hidden lever: a high wind deductible, often a percentage of insured value, lowers the premium but leaves more of any loss with the owner. Lenders set minimum coverage, which can push the cost above the seller's.",
    example: "A seller pays [[$350]] per unit on a [[240]]-unit coastal property, $84K a year. Your quote, with the wind coverage your lender requires, is [[$900]] per unit: $216K. NOI falls $132K; at a [[5.5%]] cap rate, that's $2.4M of value.",
    trap: "Premiums can move sharply at a single renewal, so run a sensitivity rather than growing insurance at inflation. The follow-up: what do lenders usually require? Replacement-cost property cover, liability and loss-of-rents cover, plus flood or wind where exposed."
