    q: "How would you underwrite payroll for a 250-unit apartment building?",
    a: "Build it from a staffing plan rather than taking the seller's total: the positions the building needs (a manager, leasing agents, maintenance technicians), a market wage for each and a burden for payroll taxes, benefits and workers' compensation, often roughly 25–35% of wages. Then check the result per unit against comparable properties.",
    why: "Payroll is usually the largest controllable expense, and the seller's figure may not transfer. A portfolio owner may share a manager or maintenance crew across nearby buildings, so a standalone property needs more people, and a T-12 can carry vacant positions that understate a full team. A new manager may also bring its own staffing model. Building from the bottom up lets you defend every dollar and see what a leaner plan would really save.",
