    id: "re-noi-029",
    track: "re",
    module: "re-noi",
    topic: "Delinquency and bad debt",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What's the difference between delinquency and bad debt?",
    a: "Delinquency is rent that's billed and past due but may still be collected. Bad debt is the part written off as uncollectible. Delinquency is a warning sign; bad debt is a realized loss that reduces revenue. In underwriting, the credit loss line should reflect both, sized from the property's history and the current aging of receivables.",
    why: "Rent is billed on the first of the month, but billing isn't collecting. On an accrual statement, unpaid rent can sit in revenue for months before anyone writes it off, so a T-12 can look healthier than the cash that actually came in. That's why buyers ask for the receivables aging, showing what's 30, 60 and 90+ days past due, and compare rent billed with cash collected. Trouble usually shows up in cash first and in bad debt later.",
    example: "GPR of [[$4.8M]]. The T-12 shows [[$20K]] of bad debt, but [[$140K]] of rent is more than [[90]] days past due. If none of it is collected, credit loss is $160K, or 3.3% of GPR, not 0.4%.",
    trap: "Ask whether delinquent tenants still count as occupied. A building can report 95% occupancy while a meaningful share of units pay nothing, which is why economic occupancy matters as much as physical occupancy."
