    a: "Remove one-time income and one-time costs so NOI reflects a normal year. Strip out lease termination fees, insurance proceeds and tax refunds, and add back genuine one-offs like a lawsuit settlement or storm cleanup. Be skeptical of anything labeled non-recurring that shows up every year: replace it with a normal allowance rather than deleting it.",
    why: "Value is a multiple of NOI, so a one-time item capitalized at a 5% cap rate is priced as if it recurs forever: $50K of one-off income adds $1.0M of value. Some items hide inside expense lines, like a refund from a prior-year tax appeal booked as negative tax expense, which makes taxes look low. Normalizing cuts both ways: a genuine one-off cost, like a storm, should be added back even though that helps the seller.",
