    classic: true,
    q: "What's the difference between in-place, mark-to-market and stabilized NOI?",
    a: "In-place NOI is what the property earns today: the current rent roll and occupancy, annualized, with normalized expenses. Mark-to-market NOI resets every in-place lease to today's market rent at current occupancy. Stabilized NOI also brings occupancy to a sustainable long-run level. Each step adds upside that takes time, money and execution to capture.",
    why: "The three answer different questions. In-place is the income you're buying, and it anchors lenders and cautious buyers. Mark-to-market measures the rent upside embedded in below-market leases, but you capture it only as leases roll, and only if the market holds. Stabilized is where a lease-up or value-add plan ends, and getting there usually takes capital, concessions and time. Splitting the gaps shows where the upside comes from: rent (in-place to mark-to-market) or occupancy (mark-to-market to stabilized). Each needs a different plan and carries different risk.",
    example: "[[200]] units, [[170]] occupied at [[$1,800]] a month; market rent [[$2,000]]; expenses [[$1.50M]]. In-place: $3.67M − $1.50M = $2.17M. Mark-to-market: $4.08M − $1.50M = $2.58M. Stabilized at [[190]] units and [[$1.60M]] of expenses: $4.56M − $1.60M = $2.96M.",
    trap: "Mark-to-market can cut NOI. If in-place rents are above market, NOI falls as leases roll, which matters most in office and retail, where long leases may have been signed at a peak.",
