    id: "re-noi-035",
    track: "re",
    module: "re-noi",
    topic: "Same-store NOI",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What is same-store NOI growth, and why do REITs report it?",
    a: "It's NOI growth for the properties owned and stabilized in both periods being compared, leaving out acquisitions, sales and developments. It isolates organic growth from rents, occupancy and expense control. Total NOI growth can't, because buying buildings raises NOI without any operating improvement.",
    why: "Investors want to know whether the existing portfolio is getting better. A REIT that grows by acquisition can post big increases in total NOI that say nothing about how its buildings perform. Same-store growth strips that out and makes REITs comparable across companies and years. It's a non-GAAP measure and definitions vary, including which properties qualify, whether it's on a cash or GAAP basis and how redevelopments are treated, so read the footnote before comparing two companies.",
    example: "Last year [[40]] properties earned [[$100M]] of NOI. This year the same 40 earn [[$103M]], and [[5]] properties bought midyear add [[$6M]]. Total NOI growth is 9.0%; same-store growth is 3.0%.",
    trap: "Watch the pool. A REIT can flatter the number by moving struggling properties out of the same-store pool, for example into redevelopment. Check the pool's size and changes each period."
